# The annotations of the game image: the single source of truth for src/game/*.asm (kit/mkgame.py).
# The format is in kit/mkgame.py, class Annotations. One line an entry:
#
#   @module NAME ; title            the entries from here on go to NAME.asm, the title heads the file
#   @about a paragraph              the module file's header text, one paragraph a line
#   $ADDR c Name ; title            a routine
#   > a description line
#   $ADDR b Name ; title            data (w words, t text, s space; "b Name 8" rows of 8)
#   @res graphics/NAME.bin          the entry is a resource: src/res/..., INCBIN
#   $ADDR Name ; comment            a label and comment inside an entry: on an instruction, or on the data line
#                                   that starts at ADDR (a data entry's rows are cut there)
#   @isub $ADDR LD HL,-6            the instruction written so
#   @equ $ADDR Name [N] ; comment    a variable past the image: an EQU, its operands by name
#   @inline $ADDR                   a routine that reads its data after the CALL
#
# The game image $5B00-$FFFF at the entry $AB4D, as the Bleepload loader leaves it (tools/image.py). Witness builds:
# case.json (c1 = work/stage2/c1g, the original tape's recording from the entry on). The code in RAM 1 (the AY player,
# src/music/music.asm) is named through @bank lines.
@names 1 ../music/music.ann ; the AY player in RAM 1 (src/music/music.asm)
@bank $C3E4 1 ; the tune start (A = the tune number); the image's $5E00 + $03E4
@bank $C46C 1 ; the frame tick; the image's $5E00 + $046C
@bank $C685 1 ; the effect start (A = the effect number); the image's $5E00 + $0685

# ---- 00-low.ann
# fragment 0 (the lead): $5B00-$6DFF, the bytes below the game's own: the ROM's, the loader's and the AY player's
@module loader ; What the ROM and the loader leave below the game: system variables, the BASIC, the second stage, the AY player
@about The image is memory at the game's entry $AB4D (tools/image.py: SkoolKit's simulated load of the original TZX on a 128K). Below the graphics at $6E00 it holds what the 128K ROM and the loader left: the ROM's paging routines and system variables at $5B00-$5CCA, the first 53 bytes of the BASIC "Rick", the second loader stage at $5D00 (its last bytes the loader's stack), and from $5E00 the AY player and its tunes, which the entry copies to RAM 1 (src/music), then two sprite frames the entry copies to their places; once copied, $5E00-$6DFF is work space (the char map, the cell flags).
@about The game rebuilds $5C00-$5CFF as its char property table (BuildCharProps $F6A9); the bytes here are the ones the load leaves, kept so the image is the original's.

$5B00 b RomPagingRoutines ; The 128K ROM's paging routines, copied here by its reset (SWAP, YOUNGER, ONERROR, PIN, POUT, POUT2)
> Known only from the 128K ROM: the load runs on the 128K editor, whose reset copies them. No code of the game calls them.
$5B58 b Rom128Variables ; The 128K ROM's system variables (TARGET, RETADDR, BANK_M $5B5C, ...) and its stack TSTACK up to $5BFF
> Known only from the 128K ROM.
$5C00 b SystemVariables ; The 48K system variables as the loader leaves them (DF_SZ 2, NMIADD 0, RAMTOP $C350, ...)
> The game rebuilds `$5C00-$5CFF` as its char property table before it uses it (BuildCharProps `$F6A9`).
$5CB6 b ChannelInfo ; The channel information: K, S, R and P, then the $80 end mark
$5CCB b BasicLeft ; The first 53 bytes of the BASIC "Rick" (src/tape/basic.asm): lines 10, 20 and the start of 30; the second stage overwrote the rest
$5D00 b LoaderStage2 ; The second loader stage, deciphered (src/loader/stage2.asm): the build's stage2.bin
@res loader/stage2.bin
$5DE7 b LoaderStack ; The loader's stack at the game's entry: 24 bytes under $5DFF (zeros on the tape)
> What the second stage's calls (LoadGroup, the ROM's KEY-SCAN) left on its stack (SP = `$5DFF` from `$5D51`); nothing reads them.
$5E00 b MusicImage ; The AY player and its tunes, $0DA4 bytes, as the entry copies them to RAM 1 $C000 (src/music/music.asm)
@res music/music.bin
$6BA4 b Kind16Frame 6 ; A sprite frame (24 x 21, 126 bytes): kind 16's graphic, which Start $AB4D copies to $6A80
> Start (`$AB62`) LDIRs it to `$6A80`, the frame ObjectKinds gives kind 16, past the map's rows: the music's bytes at
> `$5E00-$6BA3` and these become work space (the char map from `$6500`, the cell flags from `$6C00`) once RAM 1 has
> the player. Read once, by that copy (c1).
@res sprites/copied.bin
$6C22 b Kind19Frame 6 ; A sprite frame: kind 19's graphic, which Start $AB4D copies to $7080
> The second LDIR of Start, to GraphicSlot19 `$7080`.
@res sprites/copied.bin
@const CellFlags $6C00 ; the cell flags: one byte per char cell of the map window, $6C00 + 32 x row + column
$6CA0 s LowZeros ; Zeros up to the graphics: part of the cell flags ($6B00-$707F) as the load leaves them
> The game writes the cell flags before it reads them (MarkAllDirty `$A239`).

# ---- 10-e.ann
# Fragment E: $6E00-$9FEC (the cell flags' end, the char sets, the IM 2 table, the sprite frames, the font).
# Group 2 of the tape (LoadGroup $FA00) loads $6E00 on; every byte of $6E00-$70FF and $8300-$8400 is 0 as loaded.

@module screenwork ; The end of the cell flags and a graphic slot: work space after the char map
@about The cell flags are one byte for each char cell of the map window `$6600-$69FF` (32 x 32 cells): `$6C00-$6FFF`, the first half in the range before this one. A cell whose char changed is marked 1 (`$AE25`, `$AE4D`, `$AE76`, `$AE9A`, the scrolls) and the redraw `$A834` (and `$A25D`) walks the flags from `$6C00` up to the end mark `$FF` it puts at `$6F00`, the 24 rows of the screen, redrawing only the marked cells. `$A239` and `$AF18` fill the flags with 1 (`$0101` pushed from `$6F00` and `$7000` down): redraw everything.
@about Fragment E ends at the font's last byte, `$9FEC`, before the sound code at `$9FED`.

$6E00 s CellFlagsRow16 ; The cell flags of rows 16-23 of the map window (`$6C00` + 32 x row + column), 0 as loaded
> Written 1 by the scrolls' compares (`$AE25`, `$AE76`, `$AE9A`) and the fills `$A239` / `$AF18`, read and cleared by the redraw `$A834` / `$A25D` and `$A6D0`. Read and written in every recording.
$6F00 s CellFlagsRow24 ; The cell flags of rows 24-31 (below the screen); `$6F00` is also the redraw's end mark
> The redraws `$A25D` and `$A834` put `$FF` at `$6F00` (CellFlagsRow24's first byte) to stop their scan after row 23; `$A781` saves the byte into its own code (`$A80F`) and puts it back after its copy. `$AF18` fills the 256 bytes with 1 (SP `$7000`, 8 x 16 PUSH). `$AE76` scans the whole map window up to its end mark at CellFlagsEnd.
$7000 b CellFlagsEnd ; The end mark of the 32-row scan: `$AE76` writes `$FF` here
$7001 s Unused7001 ; 127 bytes no routine reads in play (0 as loaded)
> Known only from the code: no instruction names an address in it, and the recordings read and write it only while the tape loads (`c1`).
$7080 s GraphicSlot19 ; A 126-byte sprite frame filled at the entry: kind 19's graphic (ObjectKinds `$D3B6`)
> The entry `$AB4D` copies 126 bytes from `$6C22` here (`$AB62`, after the 126 from `$6BA4` to `$6A80`, kind 16's graphic): the two graphics kept past the music's bytes, which the game reuses as work space. The format is SpriteFrames'. No recording drew kind 19.
$70FE s Unused70FE ; 2 bytes, not read in play

@module charsets ; The two char sets: 256 chars of 8 x 8 and their attributes each
@about A zone draws with char set 0 (levels 1 and 2) or 1 (levels 3 and 4): EnterZone `$AC83` reads the zone's byte +0 and patches the attribute page (`$79` or `$82`) into `$A853` and `$AA80`, and passes the bitmaps (`$7100` or `$7A00`) to the char properties' builder `$F6A9`. Char C's 8 lines lie at the set + 8C, top line first; its attribute at the attribute page + C. The map (`$6500` on) holds the char codes; the redraw `$A834` copies a char's 8 lines and its attribute to the screen.
@about The editable form is src/res/graphics/charset0.png and charset1.png (tools/res_gfx.py; src/res/graphics/README.md): a sheet of 16 x 16 cells in their colours, the attributes in the PNG's text chunk.

$7100 b CharSet0 8 ; Char set 0 (levels 1-2): 256 chars of 8 lines
> Read by the redraws `$A834`, `$A25D` and `$A6D0` (the char cells under the sprites).
@res graphics/charset0.bin
$7900 b CharAttr0 16 ; The attributes of char set 0; char 0's (`$7900`) is the screen's empty colour, set by the level
> `$E320` (LevelIntro) and `$AC14` write `$7900`: the colour of char 0 (empty space) follows the level record's attribute; `$ABD8` and `$E267` write it too. Read by `$AA0E` (the HUD) and the redraw through the page patched into `$A853`.
@res graphics/charattr0.bin
$7A00 b CharSet1 8 ; Char set 1 (levels 3-4): 256 chars of 8 lines
@res graphics/charset1.bin
$8200 b CharAttr1 16 ; The attributes of char set 1
@res graphics/charattr1.bin

@module interrupt ; The IM 2 vector table
$8300 s Im2Table ; The IM 2 vector table: 257 bytes `$FE` written at the entry (`$A215`, I = `$83`), 0 as loaded
> `$A215` fills `$8300-$8400` with `$FE` and sets I = `$83`, so any data bus value gives the vector `$FEFE`, the handler. With the Spectrum's idle bus (`$FF`) every interrupt reads `$83FF` and `$8400` (every routine of the recordings shows that read: the interrupt's acceptance booked to the interrupted instruction).

@module sprites ; The 54 sprite frames: 24 x 21 pixels, a mask and pixels, 126 bytes each
@about Frame F (0-53) is at `$8401` + 126F. An object's slot holds the frame's address (+4/+5): from its kind's graphic (ObjectKinds +2/+3, kinds 4-21), from its animation list (`$EE0B`: a frame byte B gives `$8383` + 126B, so B = F + 1, bit 7 the mirror), or from the code (Rick's frames, ChooseRickFrame `$FC60`; the dynamite's FuseFrames `$ECF2` and BlastFrames `$ECF6`). A falling enemy shows its graphic + 126 (`$F099`, `$F3ED`): the next frame.
@about DrawSprite `$A419` sets SP to the frame and POPs HL, BC and DE for each of the 21 rows: the row's 6 bytes are OR3, OR2, OR1, AND1, AND3, AND2 for the columns 1-3 left to right (screen byte AND mask OR pixels); the mirrored draw (slot +6 bit 0) swaps columns 1 and 3 and reverses each byte through the table at `$5B00`. The editable form is src/res/sprites/frames.png (tools/res_gfx.py; src/res/sprites/README.md).
@about Frames 20-22, 25, 26, 30 and 31 are named by no kind, animation list or instruction: known only as pictures.

$8401 b SpriteFrames 6 ; 54 frames x 21 rows x 6 bytes (OR3, OR2, OR1, AND1, AND3, AND2)
@res sprites/frames.bin
$8401 RickWalk0 ; frame 0: Rick standing, walk step 0 (WalkFrames `$FD6D`)
$847F RickWalk1 ; frame 1: walk steps 1 and 3
$84FD RickWalk2 ; frame 2: walk step 2, and Rick in the air (jumping, falling: `$FD18`)
$857B RickDying ; frame 3: Rick dying, the mirror flipped every 4 frames (`$FC81`)
$85F9 Frame4 ; frame 4: a small shot, the bullet (the picture); kind 57's animation (mirrored)
$8677 RickCrawl1 ; frame 5: crawl steps 2-3 (CrawlFrames `$FD75`)
$86F5 RickCrawl0 ; frame 6: crawl steps 0-1
$8773 RickFiring ; frame 7: firing the pistol (`$FD29`)
$87F1 RickPoking ; frame 8: poking with the stick (`$FC95`)
$886F RickClimbing ; frame 9: on a ladder, the mirror wiggling (`$FCE0`)
$88ED DynamiteFuse ; frame 10: the burning stick of dynamite (FuseFrames `$ECF2`)
$896B BulletsPickup ; frame 11: kind 17's graphic, the bullets to pick up
$89E9 Blast0 ; frame 12: the blast, small (BlastFrames `$ECF6`); kinds 29, 30, 54, 55, 67, 68
$8A67 Blast1 ; frame 13: the blast, large; kinds 54, 55, 67, 68
$8AE5 Blast2 ; frame 14: the blast, largest; kinds 54, 55, 67, 68
$8B63 Frame15 ; frame 15: kind 20's graphic
$8BE1 Frame16 ; frame 16: kind 18's graphic, kind 47's animation
$8C5F Frame17 ; frame 17: kind 21's graphic, kind 65's animation
$8CDD Frame18 ; frame 18: kinds 7-9's graphic
$8D5B Frame19 ; frame 19: kinds 7-9 falling (graphic + 126)
$8DD9 Frame20 ; frame 20
$8E57 Frame21 ; frame 21
$8ED5 Frame22 ; frame 22
$8F53 Frame23 ; frame 23: kinds 4-6's graphic
$8FD1 Frame24 ; frame 24: kinds 4-6 falling
$904F Frame25 ; frame 25
$90CD Frame26 ; frame 26
$914B Frame27 ; frame 27: kinds 10-15's graphic
$91C9 Frame28 ; frame 28: kinds 10-15 falling
$9247 Frame29 ; frame 29: kind 56's animation (mirrored)
$92C5 Frame30 ; frame 30
$9343 Frame31 ; frame 31
$93C1 Frame32 ; frame 32: a stone block (the picture); kinds 24, 28, 31, 33, 35, 36, 38
$943F Frame33 ; frame 33: kinds 25-27
$94BD Frame34 ; frame 34: kind 32
$953B Frame35 ; frame 35: a jar (the picture); kind 34
$95B9 Frame36 ; frame 36: a row of spikes (the picture); kinds 37, 39
$9637 Frame37 ; frame 37: kinds 40, 41
$96B5 Frame38 ; frame 38: kinds 40, 41
$9733 Frame39 ; frame 39: a boulder (the picture); kind 42
$97B1 Frame40 ; frame 40: a bat (the picture); kind 43's animation with frame 41
$982F Frame41 ; frame 41: the bat, wings spread; kind 43
$98AD Frame42 ; frame 42: kinds 44, 48
