@module font ; The text font: 43 glyphs of 8 x 8
$9E95 b Font 8 ; 43 glyphs: 0-9, A-Z, three pictures, comma, full stop, question mark, space
> Glyph G at `$9E95` + 8G, top line first. The printers: `$A977` (the score and counters, digits 0-9 as numbers), `$A9E8`, `$AA0E` / `$AA25` (codes as glyph numbers) and `$AA8D` / `$AA9F` (ASCII: code - `$37`, so "A" = 10). The editable form is src/res/graphics/font.png.
@res graphics/font.bin
$9E95 FontDigits ; glyphs 0-9: the digits
$9EE5 FontLetters ; glyphs 10-35: A-Z
$9FB5 FontIcons ; glyphs 36-38: pictures (a stick of dynamite, bullets, a face: the HUD's counters)
$9FCD FontPunctuation ; glyphs 39-42: comma, full stop, question mark, space

# ---- 20-a.ann
# Fragment A: $9FED-$AF63 (sound, set-up, controls, interrupt, render, hud, levels, objects)


@module sound ; The sound: effects and tunes on the AY player in RAM 1, the 48K beeper effects
@about A 128K runs every sound through the AY player that CopyPlayerToRam1 $A069 copies from the image's $5E00-$6BA3 to RAM 1 at $C000: PlayEffect pages RAM 1 in and calls its effect entry $C685, StartTune its tune entry $C3E4, and SoundTick its tick $C46C, 50 times a second from the interrupt handler (the CALL that $A069 writes at $FF11). Each of them pages RAM 0 back and enables interrupts.
@about On a 48K (Is48K $A08D not 0, set by DetectPaging $A043) there are no tunes (StartTune returns at once, the handler's slot at $FF11 stays three NOPs) and the effects go to BeeperEffect $A08E, six tone patterns on bit 4 of port $FE. The beeper48 recording (work/stage2/beeper48: the original loaded on a 48K, level 1 played with our own keys) runs it: 98 effects, Is48K $FF and the handler's slot three NOPs in its start state.

$9FED c PlayEffect ; Play sound effect A: on the AY (128K) or the beeper (48K)
> Input: A the effect number. On a 48K (Is48K not 0) calls BeeperEffect with A (and the effect number in C, which the beeper's first OUT uses as the port's low byte). On a 128K pages RAM 1 in (OUT $7FFD = 1), calls AyPlayEffect $C685 with A, pages RAM 0 back (OUT $7FFD = 0) and enables interrupts.
> Output: AF and C changed on the 128K path; HL, DE, BC preserved there. Interrupts enabled on return. 13 call sites, all in the game code from $EC93 to $FD44.
> Witness: L1 6022 (beeper48, c1, controls, emptygun, ending2, gameover, l1z1left, spotL2Z4-34, spotL3Z7-51, spotL4Z8-69, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43, spotL1Z4-44, spotL1Z4-47).
$9FFB PlayEffectAy ; the 128K path: page RAM 1, call the player, page RAM 0
@isub $A008 CALL AyPlayEffect

$A016 c StartTune ; Start tune A on the AY player (128K only)
> Input: A the tune number. Returns at once on a 48K. Else pages RAM 1, calls AyPlayTune $C3E4, pages RAM 0 back, enables interrupts.
> The tunes the callers start: 5 the title ($E220), 0 the game start ($E324), 6 the game over ($E386), at a level's end the number of the next level, 1-4 ($AC20).
> Output: AF, BC, D changed.
> Witness: L1 31 (beeper48, c1, controls, ending2, gameover, l1end, L3Z12, level1).
@isub $A025 CALL AyPlayTune

$A030 c SoundTick ; The AY player's frame tick, called by the interrupt handler
> Pages RAM 1, calls AyTick $C46C, pages RAM 0 back and enables interrupts. Called only from the slot at $FF11 inside the interrupt handler ($FEFE), which CopyPlayerToRam1 fills with CALL $A030 on a 128K: 50 times a second for the whole game (c1: 14301 runs in 33400 frames, most of them the loading's).
> Output: AF, BC changed (the handler saves every register).
> Witness: L1 110335 (c1, controls, emptygun, ending2, gameover, l1end, l1z1left, spotL2Z4-34, spotL3Z7-51, spotL4Z8-69, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43, spotL1Z4-44, spotL1Z4-47).
@isub $A038 CALL AyTick

@module setup ; The start: the 128K test, the player's copy to RAM 1, the interrupt mode, the mirror table
@about The game's entry Start $AB4D runs once: DetectPaging $A043 tells a 128K from a 48K and on a 128K copies the AY player to RAM 1 and hooks its tick into the interrupt handler, SetUpInterrupts $A215 sets IM 2, two 126-byte blocks are moved out of the player's area, BuildMirrorTable fills $5B00 with the bit-reversed bytes the sprite drawers mirror with, and the game goes to the title at $E219.

$A043 c DetectPaging ; Tell a 128K from a 48K by paging RAM 1 in; on a 128K go on to copy the player
> Reads the byte E at $C000 (RAM 0), pages RAM 1 in, writes E + 1 at $C000, pages RAM 0 back and reads $C000 again. Still E: the write went to another page, the machine pages, and the routine goes on into CopyPlayerToRam1. E + 1: one RAM only (a 48K): the byte is put back and Is48K is set to $FF.
> Input: none. Output: A, BC, DE changed. Interrupts left disabled. Called once from Start (c1: 1 run, the 128K path).
> Witness: L1 1 (c1).
@isub $A044 LD A,($C000)
@isub $A052 LD ($C000),A
@isub $A058 LD A,($C000)
@isub $A060 LD ($C000),A
$A05F Set48K ; a 48K: restore $C000, Is48K = $FF, return (run in no recording: beeper48 starts after the load; its state has Is48K = $FF)

$A069 c CopyPlayerToRam1 ; Copy the AY player and its tunes to RAM 1 and hook its tick into the interrupt handler
> Pages RAM 1, copies the $0DA4 bytes $5E00-$6BA3 to $C000-$CDA3, pages RAM 0 back, then writes CALL SoundTick ($CD $30 $A0) over the three NOPs at $FF11-$FF13 in the interrupt handler ($FEFE).
> Entered from DetectPaging on a 128K only. Output: A, BC, DE, HL changed.
> Witness: L1 1 (c1).
@isub $A070 LD DE,$C000
@isub $A073 LD HL,$5E00

@module sound
$A08D b Is48K 1 ; 0 on a 128K, $FF on a 48K (DetectPaging $A05F)
> Read by PlayEffect and StartTune. 0 in the image and in the 128K recordings; `$FF` in beeper48 (the 48K).

$A08E c BeeperEffect ; Play a sound effect on the beeper (48K)
> Input: A the effect number, C its copy (PlayEffect). B = $FE, H = $10 and L = 0: BeeperTone toggles port $FE bit 4 (the speaker) with a black border. The six patterns, E being the half period in loops of DEC C / JR NZ:
> 0: 50 tones, the period falling 50 ... 1 (a rising chirp). 2: a warble, tones alternating between E + $32 and E, E climbing by 2 from $B0 until it wraps to 0. 6: 30 tones from $28, the period stepping down by 30, 29 ... 1 (modulo 256). $0B: 32 tones, the period rising 1, 5 ... $7D (a falling note). 4: a single tone of period $80. Any other: a sweep up, periods 1, 9 ... $F9, then one tone of period 1 (the down loop adds $F8 to 1, gets no carry and stops after one step).
> The very first OUT of an effect goes to port $FE00 + the effect number (C still holds it): for an odd effect ($0B) the ULA ignores that half wave; after it C is 0.
> Output: AF, C, DE changed. Run in beeper48 only (98 effects: the chirp, the warble, the descending steps, the falling note, the click and the sweep branches all ran).
> Witness: L1 98 (beeper48).
$A099 BeeperChirpLoop ; effect 0
$A0A0 BeeperNot0
$A0A6 BeeperWarbleLoop ; effect 2
$A0B7 BeeperNot2
$A0BF BeeperDescendLoop ; effect 6
$A0C9 BeeperNot6
$A0CF BeeperFallLoop ; effect $0B
$A0DB BeeperNotB
$A0E1 BeeperSweepUp ; any other effect: the period up 1, 9 ... $F9
$A0EA BeeperSweepEnd ; one tone of period 1: 1 + $F8 gives no carry, the loop ends
$A0F4 BeeperClick ; effect 4: one tone of period $80, through BeeperTone's RET

$A0F6 c BeeperTone ; One square wave of the beeper: L out, wait E, H out, wait E
> Input: B = $FE, C the port's low byte for the first OUT, L and H the two port values, E the half period. Output: C = 0. Called by BeeperEffect only.
> Witness: L1 999 (beeper48).
$A0F9 BeeperWaitLow
$A0FF BeeperWaitHigh

@module controls ; The keys and joysticks: the four control methods, the start, pause and quit keys
@about ReadInput $A14E turns the selected control method (ControlMethod $A14C, 0-3, chosen with the keys 1-4 on the title at $E273-$E2D1) into one active-low byte, InputBits $A14D: bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 fire. Rick's code ($F8DC, $F9A4, $F9C4, $FBC2) reads only that byte.
@about The methods: 0 keyboard (O up, K down, Z left, X right, ENTER fire), 1 Kempston, 2 cursor keys (5 left, 6 down, 7 up, 8 right, 0 fire), 3 Sinclair (6 left, 7 right, 8 down, 9 up, 0 fire). Up beats down and left beats right on the keyboard; on the cursor keys the 5 (left) is read only when the 8 (right) is not held.
@about The Kempston method never reads a port: InputKempston $A19F keeps A = 1 (the method number), AND $1F and CPL make $FE, so InputBits says "right" for ever and nothing else. The controls recording selected it: 52007 runs of $A1A4. Fire is lost too: StartPressed $A123 tests bit 4 of InputBits, always 1 here, so with the Kempston only ENTER starts a game. The re-releases (MCM, Erbe, IBSA, TOSEC [a2]) insert `IN A,($1F)` (`DB 1F`) and so fix it; the +3 disk and the hacks keep the bug (doc 5, 5.3).
@about The single-key tests return Z when the key is held: the keys 1-4 (the title's method choice), the start (ENTER, a bit 7 of port $FEFE that a standard machine never pulls low, or fire), Q (quit the game, $E3C9) and P (pause, $E3D7: wait for the release, the next press and its release).

$A103 c Key1Pressed ; Z if the key 1 is held
> Reads port $F7FE (keys 1-5) and tests bit 0. Output: A, BC, F. Title only ($E273): selects the keyboard (method 0).
> Witness: L1 6595 (beeper48, c1, controls, ending2, gameover, level1).

$A10B c Key2Pressed ; Z if the key 2 is held
> Port $F7FE bit 1. Title ($E289): selects the Kempston (method 1).
> Witness: L1 6595 (beeper48, c1, controls, ending2, gameover, level1).

$A113 c Key3Pressed ; Z if the key 3 is held
> Port $F7FE bit 2. Title ($E2A1): selects the cursor keys (method 2).
> Witness: L1 6595 (beeper48, c1, controls, ending2, gameover, level1).

$A11B c Key4Pressed ; Z if the key 4 is held
> Port $F7FE bit 3. Title ($E2B9): selects the Sinclair joystick (method 3).
> Witness: L1 6595 (beeper48, c1, controls, ending2, gameover, level1).

$A123 c StartPressed ; Z if ENTER, bit 7 of port $FEFE or the method's fire is held
> Tests ENTER (port $BFFE bit 0), then bit 7 of port $FEFE (no key: 1 on a standard machine), then calls ReadInput and tests fire (InputBits bit 4). Output: A, BC, F; InputBits updated when it gets that far.
> Callers: the title ($E219, $E2D1, $E2D7), the level text ($E327, $E32C), the game over ($E389, $E38E) and the level's end ($AC38, $AC3D); each waits for the release first, then for the press.
> Witness: L1 1149386 (beeper48, c1, controls, ending2, gameover, l1end, level1-level4).
$A136 StartFireTest ; ReadInput, then bit 4 (fire)

$A13C c QuitPressed ; Z if Q is held
> Port $FBFE bit 0. Called once a game loop pass ($E3C9): Q ends the game (to the game over text at $E35A).
> Witness: L1 43345 (beeper48, c1, controls, emptygun, ending2, gameover, l1end, l1z1left, spotL2Z4-34, spotL3Z7-51, spotL4Z8-69, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43, spotL1Z4-44, spotL1Z4-47).

$A144 c PausePressed ; Z if P is held
> Port $DFFE bit 0. The game loop ($E3D7-$E3E9): P held waits for its release, the next press and that release; the frame loop ($E417) calls it once more without using the result.
> Witness: L1 244986 (beeper48, c1, controls, emptygun, ending2, gameover, l1end, l1z1left, spotL2Z4-34, spotL3Z7-51, spotL4Z8-69, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43, spotL1Z4-44, spotL1Z4-47).

$A14C b ControlMethod 1 ; The control method: 0 keyboard, 1 Kempston, 2 cursor keys, 3 Sinclair
> Set by the title's keys 1-4 ($E27F, $E297, $E2AF, $E2C7), read by ReadInput and by the title to pick the line to highlight ($E249). 0 in the image.
$A14D b InputBits 1 ; The controls, active low: bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 fire
> Written by ReadInput ($FF, then a RES for each thing held), read by StartFireTest and by Rick's code ($F8DC, $F9A4, $F9C4, $FBC2).

$A14E c ReadInput ; Read the selected control method into InputBits
> InputBits = $FF, then one RES per direction or fire found held. Method 0 (keyboard): O ($DFFE bit 1) up, else K ($BFFE bit 2) down; Z ($FEFE bit 1) left, else X ($FEFE bit 2) right; ENTER ($BFFE bit 0) fire. Methods 1-3 from InputKempston on.
> Input: none. Output: A, BC, F; HL preserved. Called by StartPressed and by Rick's update ($F86B).
> The keyboard ran in c1, controls, gameover and ending; the Kempston in controls (and 30 times in c1); the cursor keys in c1, controls and the four level recordings; the Sinclair in c1 and controls.
> Witness: L1 1145688 (beeper48, c1, controls, emptygun, ending2, gameover, l1end, l1z1left, spotL2Z4-34, spotL3Z7-51, spotL4Z8-69, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43, spotL1Z4-44, spotL1Z4-47).
$A16A ReadKeyDown ; O not held: K
$A176 ReadKeyLeft
$A185 ReadKeyRight ; Z not held: X
$A191 ReadKeyFire
$A19D ReadInputDone
$A19F InputKempston ; methods 1-3; method 1: AND $1F and CPL on the method number itself, A = $FE: "right" for ever, no port read
$A1AA InputCursor ; method 2: port $EFFE (0 9 8 7 6), then $F7FE bit 4 (5)
$A1BB CursorRight ; 8
$A1C2 CursorUp ; 7, else 6 down
$A1CC CursorDown
$A1D3 CursorLeft ; 5, only when 8 is not held
$A1E4 CursorDone
$A1E6 InputSinclair ; method 3: port $EFFE: 0 fire, 6 left else 7 right, 9 up else 8 down
$A1F2 SinclairLeft
$A1FC SinclairRight
$A203 SinclairUp
$A20C SinclairDown
$A213 SinclairDone

@module interrupt ; The interrupt mode and the frame count
@about SetUpInterrupts $A215 points the IM 2 table at the handler at $FEFE: it saves every register, counts the frame in FrameTicks $A237, calls $E49B, runs the slot at $FF11 (SoundTick on a 128K), restores and returns with EI. The frame loop ($E40F-$E41C) clears the count and waits in WaitFrames $A22A for one frame; each scroll step waits for four.

$A215 c SetUpInterrupts ; IM 2 with a 257-byte table of $FE: the handler at $FEFE
> Fills $8300-$8400 with $FE, I = $83, IM 2, EI. Called once from Start (c1: 1 run).
> Output: A, B, HL changed.
> Witness: L1 1 (c1).
$A21B FillVectorTable

$A22A c WaitFrames ; Wait until C frames have been counted since the last wait, then clear the count
> Input: C the number of frames. Enables interrupts, loops until FrameTicks >= C, then FrameTicks = 0. Output: A, F changed.
> The frame loop waits for 1 ($E41C, after clearing FrameTicks at $E411 behind a HALT); each scroll step waits for 4 ($ADD5, $AE0C).
> Witness: L1 43086 (beeper48, c1, controls, emptygun, ending2, gameover, l1end, l1z1left, spotL2Z4-34, spotL3Z7-51, spotL4Z8-69, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43, spotL1Z4-44, spotL1Z4-47).
$A22B WaitFramesLoop

$A237 b FrameTicks 2 ; The frame count: +1 each interrupt ($FF07-$FF0B), cleared by WaitFrames and the frame loop ($E411)
> Only the first byte is used.

@module render ; The playfield: the char map, the cell flags, the sprite composer and drawers, the redraws and the scrolls
@about The playfield is a char map of 32 columns from $6500 (44 rows: $6500-$6A7F, built by BuildScreenMap $AD5A from the screen's 4x4-char blocks); its rows from $6600 are the screen's rows. A cell's flag byte sits $600 above it ($6B00-$707F): 0 clean, 1 redraw the map's char, 2 or more draw composed char n from $5CF0 + 8n, $FF the end sentinel ($6F00, below the 24 screen rows $6C00-$6EFF). The charset is at CharsetBase $A73D ($7100, or $7A00 for some screens), a char's attribute at $7900 + char ($8200 with the other charset).
@about Each pass ($E414-$E41F) ComposeObjects $A73F walks the object table $EA8E (30-byte records, +0: 0 empty, $FF the end) and ComposeObject $A2EC draws every object's sprite into fresh composed chars: ComposeCell $A6D0 copies each covered cell's char (the map's, or the composed one already there) to a new buffer and points the cell's flag at it. A char whose property byte at $5C00 + char has any of bits 3-6 set gets a scratch buffer that is never shown: the sprite passes behind it.
@about A sprite frame is 21 rows of 3 bytes, each byte an (AND mask, OR data) pair, popped with SP. Aligned on x (x AND 7 = 0) it covers 3 columns of 3 or 4 cells; otherwise each row is shifted right by x AND 7 into 4 bytes (the shift chain at $A633, entered at $A633 + 14 (x AND 7)). Bit 0 of the record's +6 mirrors the frame: the bytes of a row are swapped and each one bit-reversed through the table at $5B00.
@about DrawDirtyCells $A25D then draws every flagged cell: a flag of 1 draws the map's char and clears the flag, a composed char is drawn and its flag set back to 1, so the next frame restores the background. RedrawScreen $A781 draws the flagged cells with their attributes (after MarkAllDirty $A239: the whole screen) and keeps the HUD row's flags.
@about The view scrolls by whole cells: when Rick's y passes $CC or falls below $60 the game loop ($E42B-$E43B) calls ScrollViewDown $ADB9 or ScrollViewUp $ADF0, two steps of 4 rows (32 pixels), each moving the map, the composed marks and every object, then rebuilds the map at the new ViewTopRow $ADB7.

$A239 c MarkAllDirty ; Set every cell flag of the screen to 1 ($6C00-$6EFF): the next draw redraws all
> Pushes $0101 384 times with SP = $6F00, interrupts off, then restores SP and enables interrupts.
> Callers: LoadScreen ($ACF7), the level's end ($ABD8) and the end of the last level ($AC4C). Output: B, HL changed.
> Witness: L1 442 (beeper48, c1, controls, ending2, gameover, l1end, spotL3Z7-51, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43).
$A246 MarkAllDirtyLoop
$A258 MarkAllDirtySP ; its operand: the caller's SP

$A25D c DrawDirtyCells ; Draw the flagged cells of the screen (no attributes): the per-frame update
> Sets the sentinel $6F00 = $FF, calls MaskHudCells, then scans the flags from $6C00. Flag 1: draws the map's char (the map byte $600 below the flag) from the charset at CharsetBase and clears the flag. Flag n >= 2: draws composed char n ($5CF0 + 8n) and sets the flag to 1, so the cell is restored next frame. $FF: return.
> The screen address follows from the flag's: the low byte is the cell's, the third is (H - $6C).
> Callers: the frame loop ($E41F), the game over ($E360) and the end of each scroll ($ADEC, $AE21). Output: AF, BC, DE, HL changed.
> Witness: L1 43013 (beeper48, c1, controls, emptygun, ending2, gameover, l1end, l1z1left, spotL2Z4-34, spotL3Z7-51, spotL4Z8-69, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43, spotL1Z4-44, spotL1Z4-47).
$A268 DrawDirtyNext
$A269 DrawDirtyScan
$A2AE DrawDirtyMapChar ; flag 1: the map's char, the flag cleared

$A2EC c ComposeObject ; Draw the sprite of the object at IX into composed chars of the cells it covers
> Input: IX an object record: +1 x, +2/+3 y (from the map's $6500 row; +3 is 0, or 1 with +2 below $EB, else nothing is drawn), +4/+5 the frame (0: no sprite, return), +6 bit 0 mirror.
> Finds the first map cell HL = $6500 + 32 (y / 8) + x / 8, then calls ComposeCell for every cell the sprite covers, column by column: 3 columns of 3 cells (y AND 7 below 4) or of 4 cells when aligned; 4 columns when x AND 7 is not 0 (ComposeShifted). The drawers' IY = the first composed buffer + (y AND 7); the next column's buffer is 24 or 32 bytes on.
> The column offsets are written into the drawers' (IY+d) operands. Output: everything but IX; interrupts on at the end. Called by ComposeObjects only.
> Witness: L1 225623 (beeper48, c1, controls, emptygun, ending2, gameover, l1end, l1z1left, spotL2Z4-34, spotL3Z7-51, spotL4Z8-69, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43, spotL1Z4-44, spotL1Z4-47).
$A306 ComposeObjectAt
$A3B5 ComposeAligned3 ; y AND 7 < 4: 3 cells a column
$A419 SpriteDrawAligned ; the drawer: 21 rows of 3 bytes, IY on the first composed byte, SP on the frame
$A434 SpriteAlignedPlain
$A43D SpriteAlignedRow
$A441 SpriteAlignedJump ; its operand: $A468 (plain) or $A444 (mirrored, bit 0 of +6)
$A444 SpriteAlignedMirror ; swap the bytes' order, reverse each through $5B00
$A457 SpriteAlignedPair ; its operand: the row's first (AND, OR) pair while the others are reversed
$A468 SpriteAlignedPut ; (IY+0), (IY+d1), (IY+d2): AND mask, OR data
$A470 SpriteAlignedCol2In ; its displacement, written by ComposeObject: $20 (4 cells a column) or $18 (3)
$A475 SpriteAlignedCol2Out
$A478 SpriteAlignedCol3In ; $40 or $30
$A47D SpriteAlignedCol3Out
$A485 SpriteAlignedSP ; its operand: the caller's SP
$A48A ComposeShifted ; x AND 7 not 0: the shift chain's entry, 4 columns
$A531 ComposeShifted4 ; y AND 7 >= 4: 4 cells a column
$A5DC SpriteDrawShifted ; the shifting drawer: each row into 4 bytes
$A5F7 SpriteShiftedPlain
$A600 SpriteShiftedRow
$A604 SpriteShiftedJump ; its operand: $A62B (plain) or $A607 (mirrored)
$A607 SpriteShiftedMirror
$A61A SpriteShiftedPair ; its operand: the row's first pair while the others are reversed
$A62B SpriteShiftedShift ; the mask's fill bits ($FF), then the chain
$A630 SpriteShiftEntry ; its operand: $A633 + 14 (x AND 7)
$A633 SpriteShiftChain ; 8 steps of 14 bytes, one bit each: the step entered and those after it run
$A6AC SpriteShiftedCol2In ; its displacement, written by ComposeObject: $20 or $18
$A6B1 SpriteShiftedCol2Out
$A6B4 SpriteShiftedCol3In ; $40 or $30
$A6B9 SpriteShiftedCol3Out
$A6BC SpriteShiftedCol4In ; $60 or $48
$A6C1 SpriteShiftedCol4Out
$A6CB SpriteShiftedSP ; its operand: the caller's SP

$A6D0 c ComposeCell ; Give the map cell at HL a new composed char holding what is drawn there now
> Input: HL a map cell. Output: DE the new buffer's end (the caller steps back to its start), HL, BC, AF changed. A new buffer is ComposeCount's number n, at $5CF0 + 8n; ComposeCount goes up by one.
> If the cell's char has any of bits 3-6 set in its property byte ($5C00 + char): a scratch buffer, the flag unchanged (ComposeHidden: the sprite is hidden there). Else the flag (HL + $600) becomes n and the buffer gets the char's 8 bytes: from the charset (flag 0 or 1) or from the composed char the flag names (ComposeOverSprite: two sprites in one cell).
> Called 9-16 times by ComposeObject per object. The most run routine of the range (level4: 148302 runs).
> Witness: L1 1823882 (beeper48, c1, controls, emptygun, ending2, gameover, l1end, l1z1left, spotL2Z4-34, spotL3Z7-51, spotL4Z8-69, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43, spotL1Z4-44, spotL1Z4-47).
$A6F6 ComposeCopy
$A713 ComposeOverSprite ; the cell holds a composed char already: copy that one
$A729 ComposeHidden ; a char drawn over sprites: a scratch buffer, no flag

$A73C b ComposeCount 1 ; The next composed char's number (2 at each pass's start)
> Set to 2 by ComposeObjects, counted up by ComposeCell. The flags 0 and 1 mean the map, so the numbers start at 2 ($5D00).
$A73D w CharsetBase 1 ; The charset of the screen: $7100, or $7A00 when the screen record's +0 is not 0 (LoadScreen $ACAD)
> Read by DrawDirtyCells, ComposeCell and DrawAllDirtyCells: char c's 8 bytes at CharsetBase + 8c.

$A73F c ComposeObjects ; Compose the sprites of all objects for this frame
> ComposeCount = 2, then calls ComposeObject for every record of the object table $EA8E (30 bytes each) whose +0 is not 0, up to the record whose +0 is $FF.
> Callers: the frame loop ($E414), the game over ($E35A) and ClearOffscreenFlags ($AF52). Output: everything, IX past the table.
> Witness: L1 43008 (beeper48, c1, controls, emptygun, ending2, gameover, l1end, l1z1left, spotL2Z4-34, spotL3Z7-51, spotL4Z8-69, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43, spotL1Z4-44, spotL1Z4-47).
$A748 ComposeObjectsLoop
$A755 ComposeObjectsNext

$A75D c UnusedBuildMirror ; A callable copy of BuildMirrorTable: never called
> The same code as Start's $AB6A-$AB8C (fill $5B00-$5BFF with the bit-reversed bytes) and a RET. No instruction of the game calls or jumps here and no recording ran it: known only from the code.
> Witness: L1 0 ().
$A761 UnusedMirrorByte

$A781 c RedrawScreen ; Draw every flagged cell with its attribute, keeping the HUD row's flags
> Saves the sentinel byte $6F00 (into RedrawSentinel) and the 30 flags $6C21-$6C3E of the HUD row (into SavedHudCells, 30 LDIs each way), calls DrawAllDirtyCells, then puts both back.
> Callers: LoadScreen ($ACFA), the level's end ($ABE8) and each scroll step ($ADD8, $AE0F). Output: everything.
> Witness: L1 613 (beeper48, c1, controls, ending2, gameover, l1end, spotL3Z7-51, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43).
$A80E RedrawSentinel ; its operand: the byte that was at $6F00

$A814 b SavedHudCells 8 ; The 30 flags of the HUD row $6C21-$6C3E while RedrawScreen runs (32 bytes; the last 2 unused)

$A834 c DrawAllDirtyCells ; Draw the flagged cells and their attributes
> Like DrawDirtyCells, and each cell's attribute is set from the attribute table (AttrTableHigh: $79 or $82) by its map char. A flag of 1 is cleared after the draw; a composed char's flag is left. The sentinel $FF ends the scan.
> Called by RedrawScreen only. Output: everything.
> Witness: L1 613 (beeper48, c1, controls, ending2, gameover, l1end, spotL3Z7-51, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43).
$A83F DrawAllNext
$A840 DrawAllScan
$A852 ; its operand (AttrTableHigh): the attribute table's high byte, $79 or $82 (LoadScreen $AC8D, $ACA1)
$A892 DrawAllComposed ; flag n >= 2: composed char n
@alias AttrTableHigh DrawAllScan+$12 ; the LD D,n at $A852 inside DrawAllDirtyCells, whose operand LoadScreen sets
@isub $AC8D LD (AttrTableHigh+1),A
@isub $ACA1 LD (AttrTableHigh+1),A

@module hud ; The HUD (score, dynamite, bullets, lives), the texts, the title's colours, the level's end
@about The HUD is the screen's row 1: the six score digits at $4021, then three counters drawn as up to six icons each: bullets at $4029 (char $24, a bullet), dynamite at $4031 ($25, a stick), lives at $4039 ($26), all from the font at $9E95 (the digits 0-9, A-Z, then the icons).
@about The HUD lies over the playfield: MaskHudCells $A8BF clears the cell flags under the score and the icons before each draw, so the map never overwrites them, and DrawCounter $AA0E gives the cells past the icons back to the map (flag 1).
@about The counters are bytes: Lives $A958, Dynamite $A967, Bullets $A976. A new game sets all three to 6 ($E2F7-$E30B), a life lost sets dynamite and bullets to 6 again ($E3BB), and so does a level's end. UpdateHud $A90F redraws a counter only when it differs from its copy in OldHudCounts $A93A.
@about The score is six decimal digits, one byte each (Score $A9BE). AddScore $A9E8 adds a five-digit award to its lower five digits with carry; the awards behind the score: 50 ($F18D), 500 ($EDF3), the bonus (Lives + 1) x 10000 after the last level; one more lies at $E49A.
@about LevelEnd $ABD8 runs when the exit Rick takes points at $FFFF: it clears the play area, refills dynamite and bullets, counts LevelNumber $E4AD up and shows the next level's text (after the fourth: the bonus, then back to the game loop's game over).

$A8BF c MaskHudCells ; Clear the cell flags under the score and the counters' icons
> Clears the six flags $6C21-$6C26 (the score), then as many flags as each counter has icons (at most 6): $6C29 bullets, $6C31 dynamite, $6C39 lives.
> When Bullets is 0 the JP Z at $A8CF lands in the clearing loop with HL = 0 and B as the caller left it: it writes zeros at $0000-$00FF (the ROM: ignored) B times. Harmless; with Dynamite or Lives 0 the code skips properly. Seen in emptygun: from the sixth shot (frame 420) 139 of the 350 runs take that jump (MaskBullets runs 211 times).
> Callers: DrawDirtyCells and DrawAllDirtyCells. Output: AF, B, HL changed.
> Witness: L1 43626 (beeper48, c1, controls, emptygun, ending2, gameover, l1end, l1z1left, spotL2Z4-34, spotL3Z7-51, spotL4Z8-69, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43, spotL1Z4-44, spotL1Z4-47).
$A8D9 MaskBullets
$A8DE MaskBulletsLoop
$A8F0 MaskDynamite
$A8F5 MaskDynamiteLoop
$A8F9 MaskLives
$A905 MaskLivesCount
$A90A MaskLivesLoop

$A90F c UpdateHud ; Redraw the counters that changed since the last call
> Compares Bullets, Dynamite and Lives with OldHudCounts and calls DrawBullets, DrawDynamite or DrawLives for each one that differs (keeping the new value).
> Callers: the frame loop ($E422), the game over ($E35D) and the level's end ($ABF3). Output: everything.
> Witness: L1 42930 (beeper48, c1, controls, emptygun, ending2, gameover, l1end, l1z1left, spotL2Z4-34, spotL3Z7-51, spotL4Z8-69, L1Z1-L1Z6, L2Z1-L2Z7, L3Z1-L3Z12, L4Z1-L4Z9, level1-level4, spotL1Z4-43, spotL1Z4-44, spotL1Z4-47).

$A93A b OldHudCounts 3 ; The counters as last drawn: bullets, dynamite, lives
$A93B OldDynamite
$A93C OldLives

